WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.649
Film Courage: …Right and if I was going
into pitch this to someone and I was pitching

00:00:02.649 --> 00:00:08.790
it to the two of you and you both said “Ahhhh…we
don’t like this and this and this.”

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:14.324
So would the workable and open response from
me [i.e. the writer pitching my script] be

00:00:14.324 --> 00:00:15.324
“Great!

00:00:15.324 --> 00:00:17.529
I can come back and change it and can we meet
again?”

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:19.090
Jeffrey Davis: It depends.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:21.029
First of all…Oh, I’m sorry.

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:22.029
Go ahead.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:24.370
Peter Desberg: I would not say that.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:29.699
I would say “What parts of it didn’t you
like?

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:32.660
How would you like to see it done?”

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:40.160
Because if they haven’t started the creative
collaboration, maybe I can start it?

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.710
And so if they say “Well I see it working
much better for a younger market and I’d

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:49.760
like to make these characters mid-teens.”

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.380
I’d say “Oh wow?

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.080
I hadn’t thought of it that way.”

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.560
Then you could do this and this and this…The
more I can get you involved.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.710
Jeffrey Davis: There’s another trick that
we learned from someone who is not in the

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:08.970
book, my mentor who is in his nineties now,
but sharp as a tack.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:18.750
And he used to go in and pitch television,
like ongoing series.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:23.570
By the way, you are never going to go to a
pilot meeting and have one pilot idea and

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:28.970
you are going to have someone who is more
powerful than you or you’re not going to

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:33.850
get anywhere and you’re going to have an
agent who got you in the door unless you are

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.570
very lucky and I’m not sure that’s luck
because you do need someone looking out for

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:39.290
you and protecting you.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.690
But the other thing that Peter’s always
said (and I think this comes from his science

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:48.780
background) is try to find out what kinds
of things that producer, that executive likes.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.360
Because if you go in and you pitch them westerns
and they hate westerns, you’re dead before

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:54.370
you open your mouth.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:56.630
And that’s maybe more for movies.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:57.630
I don’t know.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:04.020
Peter Desberg: But just to follow that idea,
if you have an idea for a western and you

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.950
know that they’ve made a movie about an
orphan and that really touched their heart

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.380
then you make sure you have an orphan in your
western so that you have a way to connect.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.860
Jeffrey Davis: Or if you are like me and you’re
trying to find out what directors they admire

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.310
and then find a western that director made.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:29.750
So for example, you know they like Martin
Ritt and they think Martin Ritt is the greatest

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.090
director, find some way in I guess is what
we’re saying.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:40.380
And the other thing is, when they say “no,”
move on.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.620
Don’t argue.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.250
Peter Desberg: Never get defensive, never
argue.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.020
Jeffrey Davis: Because you never want to go
in with one idea.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:52.650
If you’re going in with one idea, you’re
asking…unless obviously, Aaron Sorkin, they

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:53.650
are going to come there.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.570
I mean, there’s a group of people they go
to.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:03.110
Pilot writing is in my experience (and I think
it’s the same as it is now), is almost never

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:04.380
done by beginners.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.450
They want to go…and it makes sense for them
right?

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:14.030
So for example, you pitch this to a showrunner,
the only way you’re going to get it made

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.709
(and I’m not talking about you in reality,
I’m talking about you as this person who

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:23.340
hypothetically pitched this), you’re going
to want somebody who really knows that they

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.940
are doing or they will bury you and they will
make a bad deal with you.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.209
You want somebody who has got some chops and
some power.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:33.800
Peter Desberg: Can we slip in a little bit
of pedantry for just a moment?

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:34.800
Film Courage: Oh, please…that’s my favorite.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.890
I don’t know what it means but this sounds
good.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.150
Jeffrey Davis: It comes from academia, like
pedant.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:45.750
Peter Desberg: One of my favorite books is
called ‘Thinking Fast and Slow.’

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:51.780
It’s probably the smartest book I’ve read
in the last 30 years.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:58.520
And it basically has the thesis that we have
two modes of thinking.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:03.340
One where we get very, very rigorous and think
very hard and deep.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.590
And the other is we think intuitively.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.850
And we make just about all of our decisions
intuitively.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:16.470
And what’s kind of interesting is that when
we are forced to think deeply, which we don’t

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.059
like to do, because it’s hard.

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:25.340
Not only are we thinking deeply, but we are
cranky as we’re doing it because it takes

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.550
a lot of our resources.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:32.620
And we’re kind of skeptical as we’re doing
it because we are forced to think hard (it’s

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.499
not in our regular way of looking at things).

00:04:35.499 --> 00:04:41.419
So when I am pitching something, how can I
keep this so they are intuitively accepting

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:43.460
everything that I do.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.259
I don’t want to put them in a place where
they have to go contrary.

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:52.540
So if I made some controversial statement
“You know, I’ve got this idea for a horror

00:04:52.540 --> 00:05:01.539
film because we know that everybody secretly
always wants to do terrible things to the

00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:03.979
people around them.”

00:05:03.979 --> 00:05:04.979
And somebody says “Wait a minute?

00:05:04.979 --> 00:05:06.289
I never want to secretly do terrible things
to the people around me?”

00:05:06.289 --> 00:05:10.039
The minute that I’ve got them at that point,
I’ve lost them.

00:05:10.039 --> 00:05:15.340
And I have to now find instances and we have
to negate them and we’re arguing instead

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.909
of finding something they are going to agree
with right away.

00:05:17.909 --> 00:05:22.949
And saying “You know, most of us like the
easy way out when things are really pleasant.

00:05:22.949 --> 00:05:27.089
And so we make decisions and are “Yeah…I
do that.”

00:05:27.089 --> 00:05:29.569
Then they are cruising along with me and I’ve
got a better chance.

00:05:29.569 --> 00:05:35.259
The minute I make them skeptical, I’ve got
a really tough mountain to climb.

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:41.599
The fellow who wrote this book (Daniel Kahneman)
is a Princeton psychology professor who won

00:05:41.599 --> 00:05:43.139
the Nobel Prize for Economics.

00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:49.969
It’s a brilliant book and I get no royalties
from it, which hurts me.

00:05:49.969 --> 00:05:51.319
But I praise it anyway.

00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:53.749
Thinking Fast and Slow.

00:05:53.749 --> 00:05:58.909
But it just makes that really good point that
anything that you are saying to somebody that

00:05:58.909 --> 00:06:04.919
seems like it’s within their realm of possibility
that they can accept without arguing, you’ve

00:06:04.919 --> 00:06:08.810
got an easier sell, then if you’re stopping
and saying “Well imagine this.”

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:10.229
I can’t see that ever happening.

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:13.710
Film Courage: I just want to come back to
something that you said and it was “They

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:14.710
will bury you.”

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:15.999
Can you go into what that means?

00:06:15.999 --> 00:06:26.869
Jeffrey Davis: What I mean by that is that
what a lot of people don’t want to see (I

00:06:26.869 --> 00:06:33.779
can’t say all people) who are experienced
is an inexperienced person (especially a young

00:06:33.779 --> 00:06:39.870
person in their early 20’s) coming in and
doing something that is above their skill

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:40.870
level.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.470
They know what you don’t know.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:49.569
They’ve been doing it…even junior executives
who have a very short lifespan.

00:06:49.569 --> 00:06:51.840
And you will always want to dial up.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.629
You will always want to bring somebody with
you who knows more than you do and can believe

00:06:56.629 --> 00:06:58.460
in you and help you.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:00.139
Those are also great partnerships.

00:07:00.139 --> 00:07:08.679
I’ll give you an example, (I also told Peter
this this morning), I had a student two years

00:07:08.679 --> 00:07:13.619
ago and I also told him “I’m going to
look like the hero of this and I’m really

00:07:13.619 --> 00:07:14.619
not.

00:07:14.619 --> 00:07:15.619
This is just my job.”

00:07:15.619 --> 00:07:18.150
Peter Desberg: He’s the hero in all of his
stories.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:20.349
Jeffrey Davis: Somebody has to make me the
hero somewhere.

00:07:20.349 --> 00:07:21.590
I’m the villain everywhere else.

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:22.909
Peter Desberg: He can start with “How are
you?”

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:25.919
Film Courage: Should I start Wind Beneath
My Wings [Bette Midler song]?

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:28.229
Jeffrey Davis: Start with how are you and
people say “Sorry, busy today.”

00:07:28.229 --> 00:07:36.710
I think that the best example of this is I
had a student two years ago who was the best

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:43.139
joke writer at 21-years old and had done a
lot of improv and very young.

00:07:43.139 --> 00:07:47.599
But she could work on (which is what you want
in “A Room”), she could work on other

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:50.590
people’s scripts and would say “You know,
this would be a great joke.”

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:53.939
And they would all come from character and
conflict.

00:07:53.939 --> 00:07:57.319
And she said to me in the beginning of the
year (because it was a 2-semester course),

00:07:57.319 --> 00:08:01.749
she said to me “I’m going to come out
of here at 22 and I’m going right on to

00:08:01.749 --> 00:08:04.479
a television series as a writer.”

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:09.559
And I said to her “I hope not.”

00:08:09.559 --> 00:08:11.069
And she got really mad at me.

00:08:11.069 --> 00:08:13.129
And I said A) You won’t be ready B)

00:08:13.129 --> 00:08:18.189
They will eat you alive and C) They’ll resent
you.

00:08:18.189 --> 00:08:19.949
So what you need to do is pay your dues.

00:08:19.949 --> 00:08:26.509
And they found this or she found this program
that Second City in Chicago had started a

00:08:26.509 --> 00:08:30.909
grad program in Harold Ramis name (in his
memory).

00:08:30.909 --> 00:08:32.130
And she applied and she got in.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:36.510
And she just graduated in May I think or I
think she had 6 months to go?

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.580
She’s got one more semester.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.810
And she is like a different kid because she
took her time.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:48.180
So I guess to go back to your question, you
don’t want to put yourself in a position

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.790
where you will get pummeled because they will
pummel you.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:56.960
They have so many people to see and they are
looking for things that they can sell.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.360
It’s still a business, no matter what it’s
on.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.490
Even PBS, people go “Oh British television.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:03.870
It’s so high quality.”

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:14.520
It is but it’s supported on the BBC so we’ll
see things like THE CROWN and DOWNTON ABBEY

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.340
and things that like which we rarely see here.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.830
Except now we are seeing it here on Netflix.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.740
So I just think it’s always a good idea
to dial up.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:29.410
Don’t try to be (when you’re young), don’t
go in and pretend to know things…get people

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:35.330
to mentor you I think is the best advice I’ve
ever been given and is the best advice I could

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.210
give.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.390
And take your time.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:41.200
You want to be in it for the long haul.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.680
You don’t want to have a gold strike and
be done by the time you are 25.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:51.220
This girl, if she had done what she had in
her head, she would have been finished at

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:53.980
25 (I think).

00:09:53.980 --> 00:10:00.430
If you look at Orson Welles career, his later
career (even though he made some great movies),

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:02.690
he didn’t have the career he had at 26.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:06.260
He was too young, but he was a genius, so
that doesn’t really count.

